  "body": {
    "id": "thm:honest-upper-band",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "There exists \\(C<\\infty\\) such that the certified profiled band \\((L_n,U_n)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathfrak B_{n,\\alpha}\\) and satisfies \\(\\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal M}E_{P_{\\mathrm{obs}}^{\\otimes n}}[W_n]\\le C r_n\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(L_n\\)",
      "\\(U_n\\)",
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(\\mathfrak B_{n,\\alpha}\\)",
      "\\(P\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal M\\)",
      "\\(P_{\\mathrm{obs}}\\)",
      "\\(W_n\\)",
      "\\(r_n\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:profiled-band",
      "def:honest-band-class",
      "prop:continuous-channel-concentration",
      "thm:paired-protected-inverse",
      "thm:certified-finite-profile",
      "ass:iid",
      "ass:strata-positivity",
      "ass:bounded-outcome",
      "ass:dose-overlap"
    ],
    "justification": "This is the constructive half of the simultaneous-band frontier and now controls width directly over the witness-certified enlarged profile.",
    "gap": "Existing ADRF and deconvolution bands do not handle blind asymmetric replicates with spectral zeros through a finite nonempty-law certificate.",
    "consumer": "Studies with two nonidentical replicate measurements can report a uniformly valid ADRF band with an auditable finite construction and worst-case width.",
    "proof_tex": "Let\n\\[\n E_P=\\{\\mathscr D_n(P)\\le\\epsilon_n^2\\}.\n\\]\nBy the established continuous-channel concentration proposition and the i.i.d. sampling assumption,\n\\[\n \\inf_{P\\in\\mathcal M}P_{\\mathrm{obs}}^{\\otimes n}(E_P)\n \\ge1-\\alpha                                                   \\tag{15}\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(n\\).  On \\(E_P\\), the true law is in \\(\\Theta_{n,\\alpha}\\), hence in \\(\\Theta^{\\mathrm{cert}}_{n,\\alpha}\\) by the repaired finite-profile theorem.  It therefore belongs to a retained decoder, and the certified endpoint enclosure places \\(\\mu_P(a)\\) in that box's interval for every \\(a\\in I\\).  Consequently\n\\[\n \\underline m^{\\mathrm{cert}}_n(a)\n \\le\\mu_P(a)\\le\n \\overline m^{\\mathrm{cert}}_n(a),\\qquad a\\in I.              \\tag{16}\n\\]\nBounded potential outcomes and consistency give \\(|\\mu_P(a)|\\le B_Y\\).  Thus clipping and the outward addition of \\(\\zeta_n\\) cannot remove the true curve, and (16) implies\n\\[\n L_n(a)\\le\\mu_P(a)\\le U_n(a)\\quad\\text{for every }a\\in I. \\tag{17}\n\\]\nThe joint measurability established by the finite-profile theorem passes through finite minima, maxima, and clipping.  Each retained decoder contains its witness, so the lower certified endpoint does not exceed the upper one; in the empty case both band endpoints are zero.  Hence \\(L_n\\le U_n\\), and (15)--(17) prove \\((L_n,U_n)\\in\\mathfrak B_{n,\\alpha}\\).\n\nWe bound width directly on the enlarged decoded profile.  Regard the square root of \\(\\mathscr D_n(Q)\\) as the norm of the difference between the empirical channel vector and the channel vector of \\(Q\\) in the Hilbert direct sum used in the definition of \\(\\mathscr D_n\\).  Since \\(\\tau_n=\\epsilon_n^2\\), the repaired profile inclusion gives, for \\(Q,R\\in\\Theta^{\\mathrm{cert}}_{n,\\alpha}\\),\n\\[\n \\mathscr D_n(Q)\\vee\\mathscr D_n(R)\n \\le\\epsilon_n^2+2\\tau_n=3\\epsilon_n^2.                     \\tag{18}\n\\]\nThe Hilbert-space triangle inequality therefore gives\n\\[\n \\left[\\sum_x\\left\\{\\int_{\\mathcal C_*}\n \\left(|F_{x,Q}-F_{x,R}|^2+B_Y^{-2}|G_{x,Q}-G_{x,R}|^2\\right)\n +|p_{x,Q}-p_{x,R}|^2\\right\\}\\right]^{1/2}\n \\le2\\sqrt3\\,\\epsilon_n.                                    \\tag{19}\n\\]\nApply the established paired protected inverse theorem with \\(\\delta=2\\sqrt3\\epsilon_n\\).  Because \\(\\epsilon_n\\asymp\\sqrt{\\log(en/\\alpha)/n}\\), it yields\n\\[\n \\sup_{Q,R\\in\\Theta^{\\mathrm{cert}}_{n,\\alpha}}\n \\|\\mu_Q-\\mu_R\\|_{L^\\infty(I)}\n \\le C\\left\\{\\frac{\\log\\log(1/\\epsilon_n)}\n                      {\\log(1/\\epsilon_n)}\\right\\}^{\\beta-1/2}\n \\le C K_n^{1/2-\\beta}=C r_n.                               \\tag{20}\n\\]\nThis is valid for every \\(\\beta>1/2\\); no certificate-complexity restriction is used.\n\nSuppose the certified profile is nonempty.  Let \\(b_-\\) attain the minimum defining \\(\\underline m_n^{\\mathrm{cert}}(a)\\), let \\(b_+\\) attain the maximum defining \\(\\overline m_n^{\\mathrm{cert}}(a)\\), and choose represented laws \\(Q_-\\in\\operatorname{Dec}_n(b_-)\\) and \\(Q_+\\in\\operatorname{Dec}_n(b_+)\\), which exist by their finite witness certificates.  From inclusion in an interval of radius at most \\(\\zeta_n\\),\n\\[\n \\inf J_{b_-,n}(a)\\ge\\mu_{Q_-}(a)-2\\zeta_n,\n \\qquad\n \\sup J_{b_+,n}(a)\\le\\mu_{Q_+}(a)+2\\zeta_n.                 \\tag{21}\n\\]\nCombining (20)--(21), uniformly in \\(a\\), gives\n\\[\n \\overline m_n^{\\mathrm{cert}}(a)-\n \\underline m_n^{\\mathrm{cert}}(a)\n \\le C r_n+4\\zeta_n.                                        \\tag{22}\n\\]\nClipping cannot increase width, and the definition of the band adds at most \\(2\\zeta_n\\).  Since\n\\[\n \\frac{\\zeta_n}{r_n}=\\frac1{\\log(en)}\\longrightarrow0,      \\tag{23}\n\\]\n(22)--(23) imply \\(W_n\\le C r_n\\) on every sample with nonempty certified profile.  When that profile is empty, \\(W_n=0\\).  Thus the deterministic width bound implies\n\\[\n \\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal M}\n E_{P_{\\mathrm{obs}}^{\\otimes n}}[W_n]\\le C r_n,\n\\]\nwhich proves the theorem without an unsupported exact-profile endpoint-proximity assertion.",
